1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,453
Every morning, Arjun rides across
Bengaluru with laddoos for thirty families.

2
00:00:05,753 --> 00:00:09,402
By noon he is tired, and many
more families are still waiting.

3
00:00:09,752 --> 00:00:12,355
One brother cannot reach a whole city.

4
00:00:12,355 --> 00:00:14,887
Laddoo Lab needs distribution channels.

5
00:00:15,537 --> 00:00:18,845
So Arjun walks into a
local store near Mrs.

6
00:00:18,845 --> 00:00:19,936
Rao's school.

7
00:00:20,286 --> 00:00:22,778
Will you keep our six-packs on your shelf?

8
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:23,678
he asks.

9
00:00:23,819 --> 00:00:25,414
The shopkeeper says yes.

10
00:00:25,414 --> 00:00:28,568
Within a month, ten local
stores sell Laddoo Lab.

11
00:00:29,218 --> 00:00:31,455
So what is a distribution channel?

12
00:00:31,755 --> 00:00:35,954
Kotler and Armstrong describe it as
a set of connected businesses that

13
00:00:35,954 --> 00:00:38,933
help make a product
available to the customer.

14
00:00:39,283 --> 00:00:43,496
The helpers in the middle are called
intermediaries, like wholesalers and

15
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:44,396
retailers.

16
00:00:44,611 --> 00:00:48,795
In simple words: a channel is the
path a product takes from the maker to

17
00:00:48,795 --> 00:00:49,695
you.

18
00:00:49,904 --> 00:00:51,856
Why use a middleman at all?

19
00:00:51,856 --> 00:00:53,540
Let's count Arjun's trips.

20
00:00:53,890 --> 00:00:57,231
Without stores, Arjun
visits every family himself.

21
00:00:57,231 --> 00:00:59,582
Thirty families means thirty trips.

22
00:00:59,932 --> 00:01:03,340
With stores, he makes just
ten trips, one to each store.

23
00:01:03,690 --> 00:01:06,594
Families pick up a pack at
the store near their home.

24
00:01:06,944 --> 00:01:08,833
Ten trips instead of thirty.

25
00:01:08,833 --> 00:01:11,963
And each store reaches
families Arjun never could.

26
00:01:12,613 --> 00:01:15,473
Put the product where
the customer already is.

27
00:01:16,173 --> 00:01:19,586
Kotler and Armstrong describe
channels by their levels.

28
00:01:19,586 --> 00:01:23,069
Each level is one middleman
between the maker and you.

29
00:01:23,419 --> 00:01:24,319
Zero level.

30
00:01:24,265 --> 00:01:27,816
The maker sells straight to
you, like Arjun at your door.

31
00:01:28,166 --> 00:01:29,121
One level.

32
00:01:29,121 --> 00:01:32,894
One retailer in between,
like the ten local stores.

33
00:01:33,244 --> 00:01:34,177
Two levels.

34
00:01:34,177 --> 00:01:36,355
A wholesaler, then a retailer.

35
00:01:36,355 --> 00:01:39,527
Many packaged snacks reach
small shops this way.

36
00:01:39,877 --> 00:01:44,331
Three levels add one more middleman,
such as a jobber who supplies very

37
00:01:44,331 --> 00:01:45,295
small shops.

38
00:01:45,945 --> 00:01:50,558
Soon, a supermarket chain asks to
stock Laddoo Lab in its stores across

39
00:01:50,558 --> 00:01:51,458
the city.

40
00:01:51,752 --> 00:01:55,855
And Dev builds an online shop,
so families can order from home.

41
00:01:56,205 --> 00:01:59,406
Now Laddoo Lab reaches
customers three ways.

42
00:01:59,406 --> 00:02:04,381
Using two or more channels at once
is called multichannel distribution.

43
00:02:05,031 --> 00:02:07,449
Where do we see these
channels at work today?

44
00:02:07,799 --> 00:02:08,699
Zero level.

45
00:02:08,682 --> 00:02:11,540
A bakery sells straight
from its own counter.

46
00:02:11,890 --> 00:02:12,790
One level.

47
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:16,089
A phone maker sells through
big electronics stores.

48
00:02:16,439 --> 00:02:17,369
Two levels.

49
00:02:17,369 --> 00:02:21,260
A soap maker sells to wholesalers,
who supply small shops.

50
00:02:21,610 --> 00:02:22,605
Multichannel.

51
00:02:22,605 --> 00:02:26,617
A bank serves you at branches,
on an app, and at ATMs.

52
00:02:27,267 --> 00:02:29,657
But are more middlemen always better?

53
00:02:29,657 --> 00:02:30,557
Not always.

54
00:02:30,901 --> 00:02:31,968
The good side.

55
00:02:31,968 --> 00:02:36,573
They reach more people, keep stock
nearby, and save the maker many trips.

56
00:02:36,923 --> 00:02:37,994
The hard side.

57
00:02:37,994 --> 00:02:41,749
Each middleman keeps a share
of the price, called a margin.

58
00:02:41,749 --> 00:02:43,891
And the maker loses some control.

59
00:02:44,241 --> 00:02:48,458
The stores keep twenty percent of
the hundred and twenty rupee price.

60
00:02:48,458 --> 00:02:50,270
That is twenty four rupees.

61
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:53,750
So Laddoo Lab gets
ninety six rupees a pack.

62
00:02:53,750 --> 00:02:56,168
Still above the eighty rupee cost.

63
00:02:56,518 --> 00:03:01,237
And Nani makes Arjun check every shelf
each week, so no old packs are left

64
00:03:01,237 --> 00:03:02,137
behind.

65
00:03:02,243 --> 00:03:03,143
The lesson?

66
00:03:03,113 --> 00:03:07,485
Pick channels that reach your customers,
and keep enough control to protect

67
00:03:07,485 --> 00:03:08,385
quality.

68
00:03:08,807 --> 00:03:09,747
Quick check.

69
00:03:09,747 --> 00:03:12,430
Laddoo Lab sells through ten local stores.

70
00:03:12,430 --> 00:03:14,570
How many channel levels is that?

71
00:03:16,470 --> 00:03:17,370
One level!

72
00:03:17,308 --> 00:03:20,717
One retailer stands between
the maker and the customer.

73
00:03:21,317 --> 00:03:22,326
So, remember.

74
00:03:22,626 --> 00:03:26,413
A distribution channel is the path
from the maker to the customer.

75
00:03:26,763 --> 00:03:30,652
Middlemen cut trips and reach more
people, but they keep a margin.

76
00:03:31,002 --> 00:03:34,278
Channels have levels:
zero, one, two or three.

77
00:03:34,278 --> 00:03:36,747
Using several at once is multichannel.

78
00:03:37,347 --> 00:03:39,880
Next time, we'll explore
channel management.
